# Mobile Offline Mode

Offline Mode is a **crucial mobile UX feature**, not an edge case. Keep three concepts apart — most
mistakes here are one of them being mistaken for another.

| Concept               | What it is                                                              | Where it lives                              |
| --------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------- |
| **The toggle**        | The user forcing the app offline. Refuses the network and **steers UI**. | `offline.mode` pref, `prefs/offlineMode.ts`  |
| **Connectivity**      | What the network is actually doing: online / device offline / server unreachable. | Derived, `net/connectivity.ts`      |
| **Effective offline** | Either of the above. Selects fallback copy, and nothing else.            | `isEffectivelyOffline()`, `useOfflineStatus()` |

All of this is separate from the offline-first data layer (SQLite + repositories), which keeps
subscribed content readable with no connection regardless of any of the three.

## The toggle

- Device-local preference (`offline.mode` in AsyncStorage via `prefs/`). Do **not** sync to the
  account, and do **not** write it from a reachability reading. Nothing but the user sets it.
- Control lives on **More**, **first under Features** (above overflow tabs / Settings / OPML).
- The switch shows **the pref only**, never the derived state. A switch that reflected a network
  reading would invite the user to turn off something they never turned on, and would flip under
  them.
- When on, show description text under the switch explaining that only downloads and other content
  already on the device are available.

## While the toggle is on — park all network

- Background sync parks the same way as a real outage (`setNetworkReachable` is false).
- API helpers refuse immediately (no token refresh, no silent retry).
- New downloads are rejected; in-flight transfers pause.
- Playback uses a completed local `file://` only — no remote enclosure fallback.

## Automatic offline is softer than the toggle

Detection exists so the app stops burning through failures and can say why. It is **not** a way to
turn the toggle on for the user, and the two do not gate the same things.

| Under the toggle                       | Under automatic offline                                   |
| -------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------- |
| Requests refused outright              | Requests still attempted — an attempt is the only reliable way to learn we are back |
| Remote streaming refused               | Remote streaming allowed: a media host can be up when the API is not |
| New downloads rejected                 | New downloads **queue** and start on restore               |
| Screens steer to Downloaded / About    | **Never** steers a tab, section, or chip                   |

Rearranging somebody's screens because their signal dipped moves the app under them for a condition
they did not choose, and moves it back a moment later. A screen that cannot work without the
network keeps attempting and shows a retryable offline-flavored error through its own chrome.

Gate the hard refusals on `isOfflineModeEnabled()` — the pref alone. Use `isEffectivelyOffline()`
for background loops that would otherwise fail in a tight cycle, and `useOfflineStatus()` for copy.

## Entering and leaving automatically

- **Exit requires proof.** Only a request that reached the server, or a successful health probe,
  returns the app to online. NetInfo reporting a connection is a hint that schedules a probe and
  never an exit on its own — it reports a captive portal and a dying signal as connected.
- **Entry is debounced.** A single failure or one disconnect changes nothing the user can see. A
  blip that resolves inside the window must leave no trace, including no delayed banner.
- Retries back off exponentially to a cap, and a deliberate user action probes immediately.
- A minimum dwell keeps the message from changing faster than it can be read. Proof that the
  network works is exempt — leaving a working app in an offline state is the worse failure.
- Connectivity is ephemeral. Do not persist it to AsyncStorage or the account; a stale reading
  restored at launch is worse than no reading.

Keep these rules in a module with no React Native or Expo imports and no clock reads, so they stay
unit-testable in node.

## Screen behavior

- Screens that can work offline **default** to their offline-compatible view (usually
  **Downloaded**) **while the toggle is on**. It overrides the remembered section/tab on navigate;
  do not overwrite the stored pref. About and other local panes stay selectable after an explicit
  tap.
- A slim warning strip sits in persistent bottom chrome above the tab bar, in order: sync progress
  → offline strip → mini player. It does not slide away on stack push. One strip covers all three
  causes, with copy chosen by cause and a per-cause `testID`.
- Screens that cannot work offline (Browse global, Search, Home Clips, …) show a dedicated
  unavailable message while the toggle is on — never a spinner, empty list, or silent failure.
- Do not auto-switch bottom tabs; the user sees the message on the tab they opened.

## The strip is the only announcement

A connectivity change gets the bottom-chrome strip and nothing else. No toast, no snackbar, no
dialog, no push notification. On a weak signal those arrive over and over, for something the user
already knows and cannot fix, and each one costs an interaction to dismiss.

Copy stays brand-neutral — "Can't reach the server", not the product name — so it reads correctly on
a self-hosted instance.

## Playback is recorded while parked

Offline listening is persisted device-locally and reconciled when connectivity returns.

- Playback mutations are written to a durable local outbox with meaningful-event timestamps.
- Replay is bounded at 500 events with oldest-first eviction when the cap is reached.
- Position updates collapse to the latest position per item before replay.
- Completion stays sticky across replay so a completed item is not reopened by stale position writes.
- Replay stops on effective offline and leaves undelivered rows queued — never delete a row that
  failed to deliver. It resumes through the existing replay sync job.

